{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1050970337#3_1900125130","relevance":2,"reason":"This NPS result is about climate change in Death Valley National Park and says the desert Southwest is predicted to become warmer and drier, which supports the area around Zabriskie Point, but it is not dedicated to Zabriskie Point or a direct historical answer for that site.","learned":"Death Valley climate-change materials describe a warmer, drier future and vulnerability of desert park ecosystems."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1050970337#3_1900125130","reason":"Inspect the full NPS document to see whether it contains more direct climate-change evidence for Death Valley/Zabriskie Point that could qualify as grade 3."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1050970337#3_1900125130"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1050970337#3_1900125130","document":"Study to look at effects of climate change on wildlife - Death Valley National Park (U.S. National Park Service) ... Study to look at effects of climate change on wildlife Study to look at effects of climate change on wildlife Contact the Park Tools  ... Species that lived at low elevations expanded their range upwards. Animals living at higher elevations suffered severe reduction in their range with no place upwards to go. Death Valley holds the world high temperature record: 134°F (56.6°C). The vulnerability of desert parks to climate change makes this work especially important. The National Park Service will be able to use the data from this study to identify wildlife species that might be especially vulnerable to changes in climate. Josh Hoines, Chief of Natural and Cultural Resource Management in Death Valley National Park, said, \\\"Predicted changes in climate across the desert southwest of a warmer and drier future make Death Valley and the park's extreme climate an ideal place to study how species may respond to those changes. For many species, Death Valley may already represent their physiological limits. This study will shed insights as to how species have responded to climate shifts over the last hundred years and help managers to protect species and their habitats for the next 100 years.\\\" For more information, please visit https://parkplanning.nps.gov/ucberkeley or UC Berkeley's Museum of Vertebrate Zoology's Grinnell Resurvey website at http://mvz.berkeley.edu/Grinnell/. Last updated:","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
