{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_690527615#0_771747216","relevance":3,"reason":"This Zabriskie Point document directly supports climate/environmental change by stating that the landscape is made of sediments from Furnace Creek Lake, which dried up 5 million years ago, showing a shift from lake conditions to the present dry erosional landscape.","learned":"Zabriskie Point is composed of sediments from ancient Furnace Creek Lake, which dried up about 5 million years ago, supporting that the area changed from a lake environment to an arid badlands landscape."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1612","reason":"The core Furnace Creek Lake query has produced another grade-3 Zabriskie Point passage; continue to find any further dedicated passages about the lake drying and climate/aridity change."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1612"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1259303777#7_2693498447","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1612","rank":3,"snippet":"changes quickly. In this instance, large crystals were allowed to form with slow cooling, only to be interrupted by a sudden decreasing temperature change that sped up the crystallization process, resulting in smaller crystals. Sedimentary Rock - Rock that formed as a result of eroded materials from prior formed rocks being deposited along ocean floors, river beds, lake beds, etc., along with deposition of minerals from the water. Over years these depositions are compacted by natural forces, later to solidify into a solid mass (rock). This is a sedimentary rock formation that's part of <cover>Zabriskie Point, located within Death Valley, California. It's composed of sediments from Furnace Creek Lake </cover>which dried up roughly 5 million years ago. Millions of years of erosion have left the sediment layers exposed. Metamorphic Rock - Rock that occurred when existing sedimentary or igneous rock was exposed to pressures and in some cases temperature changes that altered their original mineralogy. Metamorphic rock known as \"granite gneiss\".\" , \"start_char\": 3874.000000 , \"title\": \"About Minerals & Crystals - FossilEra.com\" , \"url\": \"https://www.fossilera.com/pages/about-minerals-crystals\" }"}}
