{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"furnace creek lake\" \"dried up\" \"death valley\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1809342308#4_2668232340","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1621","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1809342308#4_2668232340\" , \"end_char\": 3368.000000 , \"headings\": \"Interesting facts about Death Valley Interesting facts about Death Valley\" , \"segment\": \"It is composed of sediments from <cover>Furnace Creek Lake, which dried up 5 million years ago—long before Death Valley </cover>came into existence. The geologic history of Death Valley is extremely complex and involves different types of fault activity at various periods, in addition to crustal sinking and even some volcanic activity. Rock art and artifacts indicate a human presence dating back at least 9,000 years. Death Valley is home to the Timbisha tribe of Native Americans, formerly known as the Panamint Shoshone, who have inhabited the valley for at least the past millennium. The valley received its English name in 1849 during the California Gold Rush. It was called Death Valley by prospectors and others who sought to cross the valley on their way to the gold fields, after 13 pioneers perished from one early expedition of wagon trains. During the 1850s, gold and silver were extracted in the valley. ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1809342308#3_2668230960","rank":1,"relevance":2}]}}
